# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
# Signed updates, loaded LAST, after a board's own overlays:
#   idf.py -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.defaults.signed" ...
#   idf.py -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.defaults.revb;sdkconfig.defaults.tailscale;sdkconfig.defaults.signed" ...
#
# Releases and the owner's bench builds use this file. A build from source
# without it is unchanged: no signature check, updates accepted by image name
# only. ESP-IDF loads it only when it is named; it is not a target name, so it
# is never picked up by itself.
#
# What it turns on: ESP-IDF's "signed app verification without hardware secure
# boot". The board checks an update's RSA-3072 signature (the Secure Boot v2
# format) before it switches to it, against the public key in the signature
# block of the app it is running. A bad or missing signature makes
# esp_https_ota_finish() fail with ESP_ERR_OTA_VALIDATE_FAILED, the boot slot is
# not changed, and the board keeps running what it runs.
#
# What it does NOT do: burn any eFuse, or turn on hardware secure boot or flash
# encryption. The bootloader does not check signatures, so a board stays
# reflashable over its USB cable with any image.
#
# The images this builds are UNSIGNED: the key is not on the build machine
# (CI has no key at all). Each one MUST be signed before it is flashed or
# published (tools/sign-release.sh, tools/signed-build.sh). An app built with
# this file aborts at startup if it carries no signature block, because it
# could never verify an update: ESP-IDF's esp_secure_boot_init_checks(). Over
# the air that means rollback to the previous image; flashed by cable it means
# a boot loop until a signed image is written.

CONFIG_SECURE_SIGNED_APPS_NO_SECURE_BOOT=y
CONFIG_SECURE_SIGNED_APPS_RSA_SCHEME=y
CONFIG_SECURE_SIGNED_ON_UPDATE_NO_SECURE_BOOT=y
# The key stays off the build machine: sign afterwards with espsecure.
# CONFIG_SECURE_BOOT_BUILD_SIGNED_BINARIES is not set
